# Line endings are part of the evidence here.
#
# These material packages are verified by SHA256 over exact bytes, and they are read on a different host than the one
# that produced them - sometimes by a reviewer who is the opposite host, which is the whole point. With
# core.autocrlf=true a checkout would rewrite LF to CRLF, so the reader would hash bytes that differ from the ones the
# index lists and report a mismatch that belongs to their checkout rather than to the material. Every evidence
# directory that publishes a per-file SHA256 therefore belongs here, not just the first one that hit the problem.
mission-book/reports/REX-803/evidence/** -text
# REX-805 publishes the same exact-byte receipt/index contract across hosts.
mission-book/reports/REX-805/evidence/** -text
mission-book/reports/REX-805/evidence-repaired/** -text
# 原始编码材料按字节保存 / Preserve original encoding evidence byte for byte.
docs/encoding-evidence/*.bin -text
mission-book/reports/REX-805/encoding-preimage.bin -text
# REX-806's artifact publishes per-file SHA256 for the opposite host to recompute against.
mission-book/reports/REX-806/artifact/** -text
# The cross-host verification artifact (regenerated by Mech from the running City during REX-806's review) publishes
# the same per-file SHA256 contract. Without this line a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites its LF
# endings and the reader's recomputed digests would disagree with checksums.json through no fault of the artifact.
mission-book/reports/REX-806/crosshost-artifact-2026-10-07/** -text
# 中间日志按发布字节保存 / Preserve published intermediate-material bytes.
mission-book/reports/*/intermediate-logs/** -text
mission-book/reports/*/relocation-evidence/** -text

# Exact-byte Alien REX acceptance packet.
mission-book/reports/REX-SERIES-VERIFY/alien-evidence-2026-10-07/** -text
# Exact-byte final programme acceptance evidence and per-file checksums.
mission-book/reports/REX-990/evidence/** -text
